The remainder when $37+49+14$ is divided by $12$ is the same as the remainder when the congruences of each number mod 12 are summed and then divided by 12. In other words, since we have \begin{align*}
37 &\equiv 1\pmod{12}\\
49 &\equiv 1\pmod{12} \\
14 &\equiv 2\pmod{12}
\end{align*}then therefore, $37+49+14 \equiv1+1+2 \equiv \boxed{4}\pmod{12}$.

answer:
To find the number of digits in the smallest repeating block of the decimal expansion of $\frac{5}{7}$, we need to perform long division of 5 by 7.
\begin{array}{c|cccc} \multicolumn{2}{r}{0.} & 7 & 1 & 4 \\ \cline{2-5} 7 & 5. & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{4} & 9 \\ \cline{2-3} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 1 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 7 \\ \cline{3-4} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 3 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2 & 8 \\ \cline{4-5} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 2 \\ \end{array}
Continuing the long division:
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc} \multicolumn{2}{r}{0.} & 7 & 1 & 4 & 2 & 8 & 5 \\ \cline{2-8} 7 & 5. & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{4} & 9 \\ \cline{2-3} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 1 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 7 \\ \cline{3-4} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 3 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2 & 8 \\ \cline{4-5} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 2 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 1 & 4 \\ \cline{5-6} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & 6 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & 5 & 6 \\ \cline{6-7} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & 4 & 0 \\ \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & 3 & 5 \\ \cline{7-8} \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & 5 \\ \end{array}
The remainder is 5, which is the same as the initial dividend. This means the decimal will start repeating at this point. Thus, the decimal expansion of $\frac{5}{7}$ is $0.\overline{714285}$. The repeating block is 714285, which contains 6 digits.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{6}$

