solution.answer:
Let the determinant be
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} \sin^2 A & \cot A & 1 \\ \sin^2 B & \cot B & 1 \\ \sin^2 C & \cot C & 1 \end{vmatrix} &= \begin{vmatrix} \sin^2 A & \cot A & 1 \\ \sin^2 B - \sin^2 A & \cot B - \cot A & 0 \\ \sin^2 C - \sin^2 A & \cot C - \cot A & 0 \end{vmatrix} \\ &= (\sin^2 B - \sin^2 A)(\cot C - \cot A) - (\sin^2 C - \sin^2 A)(\cot B - \cot A) \\ &= \sin^2 B \cot C - \sin^2 B \cot A - \sin^2 A \cot C + \sin^2 A \cot A \\ &\quad - \sin^2 C \cot B + \sin^2 C \cot A + \sin^2 A \cot B - \sin^2 A \cot A \\ &= \sin^2 B \cot C - \sin^2 B \cot A - \sin^2 A \cot C + \sin^2 C \cot A + \sin^2 A \cot B - \sin^2 C \cot B \\ &= \sin^2 B \cot C - \sin^2 C \cot B + \sin^2 C \cot A - \sin^2 A \cot C + \sin^2 A \cot B - \sin^2 B \cot A \\ &= \sin^2 B \frac{\cos C}{\sin C} - \sin^2 C \frac{\cos B}{\sin B} + \sin^2 C \frac{\cos A}{\sin A} - \sin^2 A \frac{\cos C}{\sin C} + \sin^2 A \frac{\cos B}{\sin B} - \sin^2 B \frac{\cos A}{\sin A} \\ &= \frac{\sin^2 B \cos C \sin B - \sin^2 C \cos B \sin C}{\sin B \sin C} + \frac{\sin^2 C \cos A \sin A - \sin^2 A \cos C \sin C}{\sin A \sin C} + \frac{\sin^2 A \cos B \sin B - \sin^2 B \cos A \sin A}{\sin A \sin B} \\ &= \frac{\sin^3 B \cos C - \sin^3 C \cos B}{\sin B \sin C} + \frac{\sin^3 C \cos A - \sin^3 A \cos C}{\sin A \sin C} + \frac{\sin^3 A \cos B - \sin^3 B \cos A}{\sin A \sin B} \\ &= \frac{\sin A (\sin^3 B \cos C - \sin^3 C \cos B) + \sin B (\sin^3 C \cos A - \sin^3 A \cos C) + \sin C (\sin^3 A \cos B - \sin^3 B \cos A)}{\sin A \sin B \sin C} \end{align*}
Let $A = B.$  Then the determinant becomes
\[ \begin{vmatrix} \sin^2 A & \cot A & 1 \\ \sin^2 A & \cot A & 1 \\ \sin^2 C & \cot C & 1 \end{vmatrix} = 0. \]Since the determinant is 0 when $A = B,$ $A - B$ must be a factor.  Similarly, $B - C$ and $C - A$ must be factors.  Also, we know that the determinant is a homogeneous expression of degree 3 in $\sin A,$ $\sin B,$ $\sin C,$ so the determinant must be of the form $k (\sin A \sin B \sin C) (A - B)(B - C)(C - A).$
Since $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are angles of a triangle, $A + B + C = \pi.$  Then $A = \pi - (B + C).$
Also, consider $A = \frac{\pi}{2},$ $B = \frac{\pi}{3},$ $C = \frac{\pi}{6}.$  Then $\sin^2 A = 1,$ $\sin^2 B = \frac{3}{4},$ $\sin^2 C = \frac{1}{4},$ $\cot A = 0,$ $\cot B = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}},$ $\cot C = \sqrt{3},$ so
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} \sin^2 A & \cot A & 1 \\ \sin^2 B & \cot B & 1 \\ \sin^2 C & \cot C & 1 \end{vmatrix} &= \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ \frac{3}{4} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} & 1 \\ \frac{1}{4} & \sqrt{3} & 1 \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 1 \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} - \sqrt{3} \right) - 0 + 1 \left( \frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{4} - \frac{1}{4 \sqrt{3}} \right) \\ &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} - \sqrt{3} + \frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{4} - \frac{1}{4 \sqrt{3}} \\ &= \frac{4 - 12 + 9 - 1}{4 \sqrt{3}} = 0. \end{align*}
Alternatively, we can say
\begin{align*} &\begin{vmatrix} \sin^2 A & \cot A & 1 \\ \sin^2 B & \cot B & 1 \\ \sin^2 C & \cot C & 1 \end{vmatrix} = \begin{vmatrix} \sin^2 A & \frac{\cos A}{\sin A} & 1 \\ \sin^2 B & \frac{\cos B}{\sin B} & 1 \\ \sin^2 C & \frac{\cos C}{\sin C} & 1 \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \frac{1}{\sin A \sin B \sin C} \begin{vmatrix} \sin^3 A & \cos A & \sin A \\ \sin^3 B & \cos B & \sin B \\ \sin^3 C & \cos C & \sin C \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \frac{1}{\sin A \sin B \sin C} [(\sin^3 A \cos B \sin C + \cos A \sin B \sin^3 C + \sin A \sin^3 B \cos C) \\ &- (\sin^3 A \sin B \cos C + \cos A \sin^3 B \sin C + \sin A \cos B \sin^3 C)]. \end{align*}

Since $A + B + C = \pi,$ $A = \pi - (B + C).$  Then
\begin{align*} \sin A &= \sin (\pi - (B + C)) = \sin (B + C) \\ &= \sin B \cos C + \cos B \sin C. \end{align*}
Also,
\begin{align*} \cot A &= \cot (\pi - (B + C)) = - \cot (B + C) \\ &= - \frac{\cot B \cot C - 1}{\cot B + \cot C} = \frac{1 - \cot B \cot C}{\cot B + \cot C}. \end{align*}
In fact, the determinant is 0.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{0}$

